Lens: CORRECTNESS. Ruling: the checker-second FAIL is set aside. R5 attempt 2 is
accepted.

TREE STATE VERIFIED FIRST (the prompt warned of a mid-window in-place mutation).
  sha256 796f1f85b5b95d300e6544550db5a4eedfc4dd99c3bd1fd227a29352234d8d95
    internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/accounts.go
  sha256 ed0d395f12918be4cdcc99ea15f38f43e11f04d1f3025483b11c3106d8622872
    internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/accounts_test.go
Identical to the hashes checker-tests pinned, and unchanged after all my probes.
accounts.go:307 currently reads `asOf.Location()`, i.e. the correct form. All my
own experiments ran on a copy at $SCRATCH/jc; nothing was written into the repo
except this verdict.

WHAT I VERIFIED MECHANICALLY (not taken from either verdict)

1. The explicit-as_of branch can only ever receive a UTC time.
   accounts.go:212-220 is the sole place asOfSupplied becomes true, and it is set
   in the same block that assigns `asOf = t` from `time.Parse("2006-01-02", s)`.
   `grep -rn activeSetAndRecurringForProjection` shows exactly one production call
   site (accounts.go:235) and one test call site (accounts_test.go:784, which
   passes asOfSupplied=FALSE). There is no reachable path that reaches line 307
   with a non-UTC asOf.

2. The normalisation at :307 is provably the identity. Probe over 2026-01-01,
   2026-03-08, 2026-04-01, 2024-02-29, 2026-11-01, 2026-12-31, 2026-06-15 under
   TZ=UTC / America/New_York / Asia/Tokyo: every parse yields Location()==UTC and
   norm.Equal(parsed) && norm.Location()==parsed.Location() in all three zones.
   So for every input the MCP tool can receive, line 307 is a no-op.

3. checker-second's mutation claim is TRUE and I reproduced it. Replacing
   `asOf.Location()` with `time.Now().Location()` at :307 is a genuine behaviour
   change (my probe prints mutantEqual=false in both non-UTC zones), and the
   mutant survives: `go test -count=1 ./internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/` is `ok`
   under America/New_York, Asia/Tokyo, UTC and Pacific/Kiritimati, and the FULL
   repo suite under America/New_York produces zero failures. No committed test
   pins the location dimension of that boundary.

4. checker-tests' sensitivity claim is also TRUE. Inserting
   `asOfDay = asOfDay.AddDate(0,0,-1)` fails TestGetBalanceProjectionReportsCrossing
   AndTopUp and TestDashboardAndMCPProjectionAgree under both TZs. The DAY
   dimension of the explicit-as_of boundary is pinned; only the LOCATION dimension
   of a provable no-op is not.

Both lanes are factually honest. The dispute is purely about whether item 3 is a
defect in the work.

WHY IT IS NOT, MEASURED AGAINST .swarm/briefs/R5.md

a) The attempt-2 contract's required fix is satisfied literally. It demands
   (R5.md:78-83) a branch on whether as_of was SUPPLIED, with the absent case
   taking "the pre-R5 path verbatim". accounts.go:295-297 is exactly that: an
   early return of `all, recurringForProjectionDefault(all)` before line 307 is
   ever reached. Criterion 3 therefore holds "by construction rather than by
   fixture choice", which is the thing R5.md:81 attaches "by construction" to.
   checker-second's verdict quotes "correct by construction" as if the brief
   imposed it on the explicit-as_of boundary; it does not. That phrase in that
   position is the WORKER'S code comment (accounts.go:303-306), and the brief's
   own use of it (line 81) is about the default branch, which is fully pinned.

b) The brief itself relies on the parse-format invariant that checker-second
   faults the work for relying on. R5.md:73-74, describing bug 3: "Explicit as_of
   is unaffected because it parses to UTC, which is exactly why the committed
   tests miss it." The contract's own defect analysis declares the explicit path
   out of the TZ bug's blast radius on precisely that ground. A checker cannot
   fail work for depending on an invariant the operative contract depends on in
   the same document.

c) The instruction the line implements was followed exactly. R5.md:86-87 says
   "normalise as_of to a day boundary with the existing `dayOf` convention
   (balance.go:201-203) ... so the truncation boundary and BalanceAt's boundary
   agree." balance.go:201-203 is `time.Date(t.Year(), t.Month(), t.Day(), 0,0,0,0,
   t.Location())` -- the worker reproduced the named convention character for
   character, including its use of the input's own Location. If `dayOf`'s
   Location handling is a latent hazard, that is a property of the convention the
   brief mandated, not a deviation by this worker. And the stated PURPOSE of the
   instruction -- that the two boundaries agree -- is met: checker-tests' boundary-
   row probe shows inTruncation==inBalance==true across three zones, and I
   confirmed FilterByDateRange's endDay and BalanceAt's dayOf both land on the
   same UTC day for every reachable input.

d) None of the three "Evidence required" items (R5.md:89-96) names an explicit-
   as_of timezone test. The two behaviourally-live fixes both have caught
   mutations recorded (default branch -> DetectRecurringAt is caught by
   accounts_test.go:786; unconditional truncation is caught by :669, :729 and
   :786). "Mutation check per fix" cannot coherently demand that a fix which is
   the identity for every reachable input be made behaviour-discriminating -- that
   would require constructing an input the tool cannot produce.

ON THE CORRECTNESS QUESTIONS I WAS ASKED

- Can any input reachable through the MCP tool today take the mutated path? No.
  Verified at the call graph (single production call site) and at the parse
  (always UTC), across three zones and seven dates including a leap day.
- Does an untested no-op differ, in correctness terms, from untested dead code?
  Not materially. The line executes, but it is semantically inert for the entire
  reachable input domain. Neither can produce a wrong answer for any input the
  system can receive. The verdict question is whether the shipped artifact can
  misbehave, and it cannot.
- Would a correctness regression escape the committed suite? Not one in the
  behaviour R5 specifies. The default path is pinned three ways (reference_amount
  200.00 on a future-leg fixture, the tomorrow-edge fixture asserted across TZs,
  and the branch itself unit-tested), and the explicit path's day boundary fails
  two tests on a one-day shift. What escapes is only a hand-edit to the location
  argument of a no-op -- an edit that would have to be made in direct contradiction
  of the four-line comment sitting immediately above it.

The decisive consideration for me: checker-tests' probe (ii) shows that deleting
line 307 entirely (`asOfDay := asOf`) leaves behaviour byte-identical and the
suite green. So a worker who simply omitted the brief's defensive normalisation
would ship an artifact with identical behaviour and strictly fewer surviving
mutants. A standard that rejects the worker who ADDED the requested defence, and
would have accepted the worker who skipped it, is not measuring correctness. It
is measuring mutation-score aesthetics on inert code.

Standing of checker-second's finding: it is a legitimate hardening observation
and I would log it, not discard it. The cheap fix is a unit test calling
activeSetAndRecurringForProjection directly with a non-UTC asOf and asserting the
truncation boundary lands on the UTC day, which would kill the mutant. That
belongs on the R12 follow-up list (where the related byDay Location-pointer bug
already lives, R5.md:99-101) or as a nit on the next touch of this function. It
is desirable future hardening, not an unmet acceptance criterion.

Verdict: OVERRULE. The delivered code is correct for the full reachable input
domain, satisfies every clause of the attempt-2 contract, and the surviving
mutant identified by checker-second targets an operation that is provably the
identity for every input get_balance_projection can receive.
